WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.880
- That's why I think the Iraq
War has failed miserably,

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:05.470
is that democracy's not
just about elections,

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.640
it's about institutions,
it's about civil liberties,

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:10.738
it's about all of these, good education,

00:00:10.738 --> 00:00:12.300
good infrastructure.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.440
That was not, I mean,
and that was not built,

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.490
even though some argued that
was the intention of the war,

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:21.270
but as you dig in more
and you see the corruption

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:24.210
between the contractors
and the other contractors,

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:26.880
and suddenly just the money
disappears for some reason,

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.976
which was all meant for kind
of rebuilding institutions.

00:00:29.976 --> 00:00:32.643
(calming music)

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:35.870
- I'm Dave Rubin and
this is the Rubin Report.

00:00:35.870 --> 00:00:36.760
Quick note everybody,

00:00:36.760 --> 00:00:38.710
we've changed up our publishing schedule,

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:39.970
so going forward, clips are going up

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:42.530
throughout the week
and our full interviews

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:44.960
will be right here on YouTube on Sundays.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.360
And of course click that subscribe button

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.419
and turn on notifications,
blah, blah, blah.

00:00:48.419 --> 00:00:50.220
And, more importantly, joining me today

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.130
is the founder of Ideas Beyond Borders,

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.260
a brand new American citizen,

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.630
Faisal Al Mutar, welcome
back to the Rubin Report.

00:00:58.630 --> 00:00:59.770
- Hello Dave, how are you?

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:02.040
- Good, I'm fine, thank you for asking,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:03.840
I dropped the Saeed there,

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.894
I said Faisal Al Mutar, do you
go by Faisal Saeed Al Mutar,

00:01:06.894 --> 00:01:08.560
Faisal Al-Mutar?

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.490
I'm actually not sure.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:12.230
- I go with Faisal Saeed
for a reason, I mean,

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:13.760
Saeed is my dad's name.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:14.593
- Yeah.

00:01:14.593 --> 00:01:15.580
- And the reason I kept it

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:18.590
is because I have a very strong
relationship with my dad.

00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:21.630
And the reason why he raised
me to be the person that I am.

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.870
I mean some people keep
their dad's name away,

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:25.980
but I kept my dad's name,
and actually my first name,

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.210
so I can maintain my
relationship with him.

00:01:28.210 --> 00:01:29.620
- Alright, then I feel
I should look at you

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:30.700
and do that intro again,

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.810
welcome to the show,
Faisal Saeed Al Mutar.

00:01:32.810 --> 00:01:33.860
- Thanks, Dave, thank you.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.640
- I am very excited to
have you here my friend,

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.170
because as of right now,

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.350
you are now a five time
Rubin Report guest.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.290
I believe that puts you

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:44.130
in the Jordan Peterson,
Ben Shapiro category.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:44.963
- Wow.

00:01:44.963 --> 00:01:45.940
- And you.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:46.800
- Oh.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.807
- A recent immigrant to the United States,

00:01:48.807 --> 00:01:49.640
can you believe it?

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:50.473
- I think I've made it.

00:01:50.473 --> 00:01:53.370
That's it, I'm out of this country.

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:56.880
Actually, what I just, I
was just noticed is that,

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.660
you really notice all my development

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:01.530
since I came to the
States, you actually like,

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:04.180
if people, if your audience
watched the previous episodes

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.410
to now, they will see like,

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:08.740
pretty much my, I think you interviewed me

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.823
one year after I get my green card.

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:13.640
- So this was around probably...

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.830
- 2014 I think, 2014, 2015?

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:18.220
- No it was 2015, probably, fall of 2015.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:20.140
- Yeah, and that was one year
after I get my green card,

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:21.440
which is 2014.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.580
I landed in Los Angeles in 2013.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:26.720
And then, yeah, and then here I am.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.820
I don't know, it's like, it's
so kind of mind boggling.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:31.140
- Yeah, well we'll have a playlist

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:32.760
of just the interviews
that I've done with you,

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:34.130
and it is an interesting evolution,

00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:36.340
we're gonna talk a little
bit about your story,

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:37.370
and all that.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:40.530
But it's funny because when you
were on the way this morning

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.190
and I was thinking about
what I wanted to talk about,

00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:43.170
and Ideas Beyond Borders

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.530
and everything else that you're doing.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:46.660
You feel to me like an old friend.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:48.560
But it's funny because
we only know each other

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:49.939
for four or five years,

00:02:49.939 --> 00:02:52.100
and a lot of what we know
about each other is public,

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.780
although, you know,
we've been out to dinner,

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:54.613
and all that so many times.

00:02:54.613 --> 00:02:56.470
But it shows you how
fast things are changing,

00:02:56.470 --> 00:02:57.600
because it does feel,

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.545
this feels like an old friendship to me.

00:02:59.545 --> 00:03:00.450
Which is weird,

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.320
because it's not like we've
known each other for 30 years.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:05.446
- I mean five years is
one fifth of my age.

00:03:05.446 --> 00:03:06.460
(laughing)

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:09.390
But yeah, I've known you since, yeah,

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.640
since I came to the United
States almost, yeah.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:12.860
- Alright, so let's--

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:14.800
- And I hope the friendship continues.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:15.633
- Well, let's just see how that goes.

00:03:15.633 --> 00:03:16.920
- Yeah, let's see about tonight.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.760
- Yeah, okay, so, first off,

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:20.580
you are a brand new American citizen

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:21.896
and I wanna talk about that.

00:03:21.896 --> 00:03:22.897
- [Faisal] Proudly so.

00:03:22.897 --> 00:03:24.170
- You've got the leather jacket to match.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:26.550
You are loving America right now.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:28.140
But, for people who
don't know who you are,

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:29.660
because obviously this
show has grown a lot

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:30.538
since way back when.

00:03:30.538 --> 00:03:31.371
- I know, yeah.
- Could we just

00:03:31.371 --> 00:03:33.670
talk a little bit about
your story in Iraq,

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:35.560
growing up in Iraq, and the
war, and everything else.

00:03:35.560 --> 00:03:37.680
Let's do a quick recap on that.

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:40.570
- Well, I mean, I was born in Babylon.

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:41.403
Babylon, Iraq.

00:03:41.403 --> 00:03:42.309
And then my--

00:03:42.309 --> 00:03:43.210
- [Dave] What year?

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:44.485
- That was 1991.

00:03:44.485 --> 00:03:45.318
- [Dave] Okay.

00:03:45.318 --> 00:03:46.440
- So right after the first Gulf War

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:47.718
and enter the sanctions.

00:03:47.718 --> 00:03:49.300
- God, you're a young kid.

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:50.620
- Well, when you hear the story,

00:03:50.620 --> 00:03:53.010
you'll see that I'm now more,
more mature than average,

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:54.880
but, and...

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:58.860
So I was born in 1991,
and then my parents moved

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.520
to Baghdad as I was
seven or eight years old.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.200
And my dad's an orthopedic surgeon,

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:05.050
my mom is a lawyer.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:08.610
So I come from, my dad stayed in the UK.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:11.030
And came back to the country to fix Iraq,

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:13.500
but then Saddam Hussein
went to the Gulf War,

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:15.910
and then that country went downhill.

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:17.780
- Were you born during
the Gulf War, actually?

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:18.613
- No, after.

00:04:18.613 --> 00:04:20.453
- So the Gulf War was 90.

00:04:20.453 --> 00:04:24.793
- In 1990, and then, so
I, and then, after that,

00:04:26.075 --> 00:04:29.327
from the Iraq side, we
declared that the war has won,

00:04:29.327 --> 00:04:31.427
and it's called the mother of all battles.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:36.270
So, that happened, and then,

00:04:36.270 --> 00:04:39.550
as I was growing up, I grew
up under Saddam Hussein, and,

00:04:39.550 --> 00:04:41.470
I mean one of the main anecdotes that I,

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:43.600
is actually very relevant to
what I was saying is that,

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.400
I didn't know that Iraq invaded Kuwait.

00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:49.910
The way that it was
manufactured by both the state,

00:04:49.910 --> 00:04:51.900
and then there was a
mosque next to our house,

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:53.110
called the mother of all battles,

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:55.600
with the menorahs designed
as a ballistic missile,

00:04:55.600 --> 00:04:56.670
and an AK-47.

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:57.503
- [Dave] Wow.

00:04:57.503 --> 00:04:58.970
- So Saddam Hussein
started building mosques

00:04:58.970 --> 00:04:59.830
all over the country.

00:04:59.830 --> 00:05:01.821
- With missiles,
basically, as the ornament

00:05:01.821 --> 00:05:03.804
at the top of the mosque.

00:05:03.804 --> 00:05:06.940
- So that was like,
and declared Iraq to be

00:05:06.940 --> 00:05:08.860
kind of an Islamic state,

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:10.770
which brings us to the future,

00:05:10.770 --> 00:05:13.870
but he's the one who put God
is great on the flag, so,

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:15.530
the environment I was growing up in,

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:17.250
even though the misconception, generally,

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:19.530
is like, Saddam used to be
secular and all of that.

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.250
I grew up in an era
called the faith campaign,

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:23.690
in which Saddam actually,
Saddam Hussein moved Iraq

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:26.070
to become a more religious state.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:27.480
- So before you were born, though,

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:28.960
so when your parents were younger,

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:30.970
it was more of a secular,

00:05:30.970 --> 00:05:34.060
or Saddam was more secular
than after the war.

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:36.960
- Relatively, yeah, and it
was more Arab nationalists,

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.180
so Arab nationalism was
kind of the phase that,

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:41.340
first Saddam moved in, it
was in a war with Iran,

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.980
and we had to differentiate ourselves

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:44.620
as the Arabs versus the Persians,

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.310
and the Iranians, all of that.

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:47.750
Actually the battle's called Khafji,

00:05:47.750 --> 00:05:50.310
which is also relevant
to the battles, anyway.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:53.760
So, 2003 happened, that's
where the Iraq war.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.850
So the area that I used to live in

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:58.030
was an area in which
many of Saddam generals

00:05:58.030 --> 00:05:59.100
used to live in.

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.150
So, as the war happened, and
Iraqi military was defeated,

00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:04.160
obviously, many of these generals

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.050
start leaving the district.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:08.040
And houses became all empty.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.040
So from suddenly a full residential area

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.760
with a lot of people, suddenly
it's like many of the houses

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:16.160
were on the highway, totally all empty.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:17.300
- Meaning they were going to fight,

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:18.133
or they were fleeing?

00:06:18.133 --> 00:06:19.010
- No, they were fleeing the country.

00:06:19.010 --> 00:06:20.660
Many of them either went to Jordan

00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:22.650
or some other Arab
state, some of them went

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.670
different provinces,
we don't know honestly.

00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:26.400
But I know that they left.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.780
And, so, after these houses became empty

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:32.970
and now suddenly chaos start
erupting in the country.

00:06:32.970 --> 00:06:35.940
These houses start being
taken over by Al Qaeda.

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:38.870
So from normal families into now militias

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:40.023
walking the streets.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.900
- So what was that really like?

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:44.750
So now you're in your
community where you live,

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:46.850
and suddenly okay, now
there's abandoned houses,

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:48.020
you don't know where these people went,

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:49.390
they departed the army.

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:50.670
And now Al Qaeda shows up,

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:52.320
I mean what is that actually like?

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:55.150
- So what it's like is what I think

00:06:55.150 --> 00:06:58.070
many people have seen in videos of

00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:00.210
when these militias take over.

00:07:00.210 --> 00:07:02.460
The usual day was,

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:05.060
there is an IED, so an
IED is an explosive device

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:06.620
that they put in the street.

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:08.250
So when there is a US Humvee comes in

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:11.030
or eventually Iraqi army starts coming up,

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:12.700
blow up and then kill some soldiers,

00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:14.067
and then there's a fight
back and all of this.

00:07:14.067 --> 00:07:16.100
Now that's not the usual day,

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:18.400
that's what people do on the weekend.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.640
And if, I mean, from my end,

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:23.570
I mean I've seen, I mean I've seen it all.

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:28.570
From people firing rockets on US tanks to

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.580
beheadings, to dead people on the street.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.690
So that was my, that's why,

00:07:34.690 --> 00:07:35.940
that was my high school era.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:37.868
- So you're seeing that at like,

00:07:37.868 --> 00:07:40.290
basically like 14 years old, 15 years old,

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:41.690
something like that, like,

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:43.940
I mean, what does that do
to a 14 year old's mind?

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:45.790
Like what did you think was happening?

00:07:45.790 --> 00:07:47.290
- I'll talk about that actually,

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:48.990
after moving to a safer area,

00:07:48.990 --> 00:07:50.870
and kind of getting that,

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:53.040
actually, maybe I can
say it now is that I,

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:56.350
just a year ago, was
just sitting in my patio,

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:58.150
my friend's patio, and,

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:00.000
it just came into me as like,

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.210
wow, I've been safe for a while.

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:03.320
Like this is,

00:08:03.320 --> 00:08:04.889
this normal life looks cool.

00:08:04.889 --> 00:08:06.080
(laughs)

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:09.530
This kind of, because
the usual day is, I mean,

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:12.170
I mean, my mom was
obviously tortured as well,

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:13.490
is that she never know

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:15.690
whether her kid is gonna
come back to house.

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:20.380
I've had this realization
actually kinda recently is like,

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:25.250
This normal life, which is
like, not to worry about

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:29.020
bombings and suicide
attacks and all of that.

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:32.280
At the time was my
normal, that was actually,

00:08:32.280 --> 00:08:35.150
and after a while, somebody
get desensitized, honestly,

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:37.580
I mean, after you
constantly start seeing it.

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:40.397
And the whole environment is
like that, it's like yeah,

00:08:40.397 --> 00:08:42.130
you talk to a friend he's like oh I just,

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:43.450
my brother got killed.

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:44.350
Oh, okay.

00:08:44.350 --> 00:08:45.426
Wanna grab lunch?

00:08:45.426 --> 00:08:48.030
Seriously, this is actually the,

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:49.393
the mood of the day.

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:53.370
So that was, in a way, these were like,

00:08:53.370 --> 00:08:55.330
the first two years were the good years.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:08:58.400
So even though there were
IEDs and some suicide bombers

00:08:58.400 --> 00:08:59.660
there were the good days.

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.880
And then there was a major
day happened in Iraq,

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:05.630
in which Al Qaeda blew up one
of the largest Shia mosques

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:06.570
in the country.

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:07.450
It's called (speaking foreign language)

00:09:07.450 --> 00:09:09.520
which is an area in Northern Baghdad.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:14.040
And as a result, now the Shia
militias start popping up.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:17.330
And my neighborhood, luckily,
was just in the middle,

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:18.940
between these two.

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:22.570
So, they start firing
rockets at each other.

00:09:22.570 --> 00:09:24.730
And that rocket can,
and most of these people

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:27.390
are inexperienced, so, the
rocket can hit a school,

00:09:27.390 --> 00:09:30.360
it can hit a house, it can, so, again,

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:33.607
the usual days, oh,
somebody's house was blown up.

00:09:33.607 --> 00:09:34.440
Oh, okay.

00:09:34.440 --> 00:09:36.600
- Can you quickly explain
the Sunni, Shia split there,

00:09:36.600 --> 00:09:39.140
'cause I think somebody might hear, oh,

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:41.314
Al Qaeda blew up a Sunni mosque,

00:09:41.314 --> 00:09:42.147
a Shia mosque.
- A Shia mosque,

00:09:42.147 --> 00:09:42.980
yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:44.890
- And not understand
why they would do that.

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:47.700
- Well, I mean, the history's long, but,

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:50.293
at least in the modern context.

00:09:51.820 --> 00:09:54.460
So Saddam Hussein used to be Sunni.

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:56.500
Which is relevant in this conversation.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:59.700
So after, so many of the
generals and the military

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:01.500
used to be from the Sunni,

00:10:01.500 --> 00:10:03.900
at least the leadership were
most of them were Sunnis.

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:07.740
So that created, and they
are the minority in Iraq.

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.170
They represent.

00:10:09.170 --> 00:10:11.360
And obviously some of them were dissident

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:13.390
against Saddam Hussein,
but generally speaking,

00:10:13.390 --> 00:10:17.270
the perception is that
Sunnis were controlling.

00:10:17.270 --> 00:10:20.440
After that, then the Iraq
started getting divided

00:10:20.440 --> 00:10:22.940
into militias, so, the
Iraqi civil identity

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:23.900
started being destroyed.

00:10:23.900 --> 00:10:26.590
So most people sort of
went back to their religion

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:28.680
and their sect, mainly their sect.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:31.500
And the Shia militias, I mean,

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:33.410
the main funder of that,

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:35.680
funding is really much,
very relevant here.

00:10:35.680 --> 00:10:38.220
Funding of the Shia
militias come from Iran.

00:10:38.220 --> 00:10:39.670
And the funding from the Sunni militias

00:10:39.670 --> 00:10:42.450
come from Qatar and Saudi
Arabia and the gulf states.

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:44.570
And they have a different,

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:46.340
in a way it's a power struggle.

00:10:46.340 --> 00:10:47.810
Who actually controls the country

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:50.908
and what vision they have to,

00:10:50.908 --> 00:10:53.160
the concept within the Sunni,

00:10:53.160 --> 00:10:56.020
within the Sunni militias is
the concept of the caliphate,

00:10:56.020 --> 00:10:58.510
which is the ISISes and Al Qaedas.

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:01.160
And within the Shias is the
(speaking foreign language),

00:11:01.160 --> 00:11:04.090
which is that the Shias have a version

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:05.743
which is kind of similar
to Christianity in a way

00:11:05.743 --> 00:11:08.470
that there was a 12th man who was hidden,

00:11:08.470 --> 00:11:11.790
and the (speaking foreign
language) now in Iran,

00:11:11.790 --> 00:11:14.360
is now kind of the representative
of that human on earth,

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:15.920
until he comes back.

00:11:15.920 --> 00:11:18.590
So in a way they're both
theocratic authoritarian,

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:20.900
but they have a different vision of what

00:11:20.900 --> 00:11:23.410
theocratic authoritarianism
looks like in practice.

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.210
- And where the funding goes,

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:26.850
which I wanna talk a
little bit more later,

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:28.652
especially related to Qatar
is really interesting.

00:11:28.652 --> 00:11:30.100
- Yeah, and yeah, funding.

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:33.507
So I've written an article
when I was in Iraq,

00:11:33.507 --> 00:11:35.890
and later on that I translated, which is,

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:37.730
that it's easier to
start a terrorist group

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:39.627
in the Middle East than
to start a liberal one.

00:11:39.627 --> 00:11:40.701
(laughing)

00:11:40.701 --> 00:11:41.700
- [Dave] For sure.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:43.092
- And many of the reasons is that,

00:11:43.092 --> 00:11:46.430
I've seen funding going
like in front of me,

00:11:46.430 --> 00:11:47.720
like I've been to a restaurant

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:49.740
and a guy came in with a bag of cash,

00:11:49.740 --> 00:11:51.620
giving it to somebody, and he's like, oh,

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:55.750
go fight with the Mujahideen
and all of that, so, it was,

00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:57.170
some of the funding was really public,

00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:58.763
it was not really like, hidden.

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:00.590
So yeah.

00:12:00.590 --> 00:12:03.470
So, that happened, and then suddenly,

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:06.400
Iraq erupted to a much
more sectarian civil war,

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:07.680
at the beginning it was,

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:10.640
we used to lose maybe 5,000 a month,

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:14.610
or now it became, recent months,
reached 25,000 or 30,000.

00:12:14.610 --> 00:12:17.460
And that's where I lost
my brother and my cousin.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:20.800
That was the peak of 2007,

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:24.000
that was when things were erupting.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:26.633
And my crazy me at the time,

00:12:26.633 --> 00:12:29.540
were like oh, why don't
everybody get along attitude,

00:12:29.540 --> 00:12:33.180
and I was with my friends and we're more

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:34.800
on the liberal side.

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:37.000
- So you were like,
starting atheist groups

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:37.833
at that time, right?

00:12:37.833 --> 00:12:41.221
- Not atheist, but mostly
like, secular, anti,

00:12:41.221 --> 00:12:44.730
anti theocracy, I mean
we had like some people

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:46.550
who were religious with us,

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:49.250
who were also antagonistic
or saw a different version

00:12:49.250 --> 00:12:51.840
of Islam, or not necessarily
believe in it that much.

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:53.790
So yes it was kind of more inclusive

00:12:53.790 --> 00:12:55.480
than just non believers.

00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:56.313
- What was that like though,

00:12:56.313 --> 00:12:58.480
to be starting a group
or just be around people

00:12:58.480 --> 00:12:59.950
that were doing something
that's pretty subversive,

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:01.080
relative to what's happening?

00:13:01.080 --> 00:13:02.730
- I mean first it started online.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:07.090
That's typically how we
find ourselves, is online,

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:09.570
when Saddam was in power, we didn't have,

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:11.380
we hardly all had two state television

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:12.940
and barely no internet.

00:13:12.940 --> 00:13:15.720
Used to call it intranet
which is only local internet.

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:17.960
And then, when the internet shows up,

00:13:17.960 --> 00:13:20.430
which is after the war,

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:22.230
then everything was opened up.

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:24.520
So Iraq, at the time,
the internet was as open

00:13:24.520 --> 00:13:25.580
as it is here in the United States,

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:27.470
you can access every
website and everything.

00:13:27.470 --> 00:13:30.830
And at the time there was
a website called Blogspot,

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:33.870
which is kind of part of
Google, Google bot, and then,

00:13:33.870 --> 00:13:37.970
through other like, forums,
and that's what I started.

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:39.843
And then after a while, like,
okay, where do you live,

00:13:39.843 --> 00:13:42.874
are you in Baghdad,
are you here, and then,

00:13:42.874 --> 00:13:45.240
many times people don't
reveal their identity

00:13:45.240 --> 00:13:46.353
for obvious reasons.

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:50.150
But I was able to, with also,

00:13:50.150 --> 00:13:53.610
from some of my friends there
were able to formulate groups

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:56.670
in which we meet up and discuss things.

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:58.310
Unfortunately after a while,

00:13:58.310 --> 00:14:00.770
that started becoming a target.

00:14:00.770 --> 00:14:03.060
So we went back to hiding.

00:14:03.060 --> 00:14:06.610
And I, after the loss of
my brother and my cousin,

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:09.790
and getting multiple death
threats, I left the country.

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:12.220
So I left Iraq in 2009.

00:14:12.220 --> 00:14:13.720
I left to Lebanon.

00:14:13.720 --> 00:14:15.250
So that's the second phase of my life,

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:17.150
in which post-Iraq, which is I left,

00:14:17.150 --> 00:14:19.290
I remember the day I left
Iraq, it was nine, nine,

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:20.480
September 2009.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:22.405
So September 9th, 2009.

00:14:22.405 --> 00:14:26.670
And Iraq at the time was
still in its war zone.

00:14:26.670 --> 00:14:30.090
There was nothing much happening, and--

00:14:30.090 --> 00:14:31.250
- I know I've asked you this before,

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:32.370
but can you just talk a little bit about,

00:14:32.370 --> 00:14:34.190
how did you get over
the border and all that?

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:36.600
- So I didn't have a quarrel
with the Iraqi government.

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:38.960
So actually I left
through the usual airport.

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:40.760
The Iraqi, it used to be called

00:14:40.760 --> 00:14:41.890
Saddam International Airport,

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:44.460
it became eventually Baghdad
International Airport.

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:47.280
So I was okay to leave the
country from this state,

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:48.900
because the state didn't have any,

00:14:48.900 --> 00:14:50.700
even though the state was sectarian,

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:52.820
but, I mean I'm the last of their worries.

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:54.690
I wasn't really a target.

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:58.950
But my neighborhood, the
whole part of West Baghdad,

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:02.270
where I come from, there
were parts, for example,

00:15:02.270 --> 00:15:05.400
(speaking foreign language),
which means, Afghan street.

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:08.410
So these are all Afghans,
came from Afghanistan,

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:10.490
who came and fought in Iraq against

00:15:10.490 --> 00:15:12.500
either US military or the government.

00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:13.900
There were like Chechnyans,

00:15:13.900 --> 00:15:15.730
and there were a lot of
people from all these,

00:15:15.730 --> 00:15:17.833
so we became like the
Times Square of terrorism.

00:15:17.833 --> 00:15:18.690
(laughing)

00:15:18.690 --> 00:15:21.980
So whatever you think of,
whatever nation that had

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:23.880
foreign fighters, they all came.

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:26.097
And some of them actually
came before the war.

00:15:26.097 --> 00:15:29.190
And when the war, when the war ended,

00:15:29.190 --> 00:15:31.360
I remember I saw my first American,

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:35.000
which was an Abrams tank
coming in front of my house.

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:37.680
And that was in April 2003.

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:38.690
- And how did you feel then?

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:40.570
'Cause this is where you have
an interesting perspective,

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:43.420
I think, on American foreign
policy relative to all this.

00:15:43.420 --> 00:15:45.590
So you see the tank going
by, what are you thinking?

00:15:45.590 --> 00:15:49.480
- It's, I mean, first,
it's definitely scary.

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:52.910
But at the same time, it
was a feeling of relief.

00:15:52.910 --> 00:15:57.750
I mean the life of the last
years of Saddam Hussein were,

00:15:57.750 --> 00:16:00.410
because he knew at the time
he was going to lose power

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:01.243
of some sort.

00:16:01.243 --> 00:16:03.690
So he was more authoritarian than usual.

00:16:03.690 --> 00:16:06.560
And the sanctions were
hitting stronger on Iraq

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:07.870
more and more to the way that

00:16:07.870 --> 00:16:11.710
Iraq economy was destroyed,
almost destroyed.

00:16:11.710 --> 00:16:13.790
So it was kind of a relief.

00:16:13.790 --> 00:16:15.900
I mean what is there to lose,
really kind of attitude,

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:18.700
is that, well maybe these
guys are gonna make it better.

00:16:18.700 --> 00:16:21.570
And my parents, even
though did not publicly,

00:16:21.570 --> 00:16:23.750
were opposed to Saddam Hussein,

00:16:23.750 --> 00:16:27.060
but they were not a
fan, they were not fans.

00:16:27.060 --> 00:16:30.490
'Cause both of them were
educated, and they wanted,

00:16:30.490 --> 00:16:32.210
they had a lot of skills
that they could contribute

00:16:32.210 --> 00:16:33.760
to make the country a better place.

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:35.680
And he kept going from war to war,

00:16:35.680 --> 00:16:37.371
destroying the Iraqi economy.

00:16:37.371 --> 00:16:39.470
Destroying Iraq in general,

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:43.090
killing tens of people,
hundreds of thousands of people.

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:44.940
- When the sanctions were
happening before the war,

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:46.990
were there people that
basically were like,

00:16:46.990 --> 00:16:48.810
if he would just change his tune,

00:16:48.810 --> 00:16:51.410
if he would stop doing certain things,

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:52.980
then America would ease up,

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:54.400
or the international
community would ease up

00:16:54.400 --> 00:16:56.210
on the sanctions, and things
would actually get better,

00:16:56.210 --> 00:16:58.390
like he sort of made his bed
is what I'm saying, right?

00:16:58.390 --> 00:17:00.160
- Yeah, I mean, it depends who you ask.

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:01.590
I mean if you ask the Kurds,

00:17:01.590 --> 00:17:04.340
they will tell you like he
gassed us multiple times.

00:17:04.340 --> 00:17:06.820
There's no negotiation here.

00:17:06.820 --> 00:17:11.150
If you ask some people who are
having relatively safer life.

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:14.660
And also, we didn't know
what the alternative was,

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:16.000
I mean now many people say oh,

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:18.680
we wish Saddam was back,
or Saddam used to be safe.

00:17:18.680 --> 00:17:20.770
But at the time, many
people didn't know that Iraq

00:17:20.770 --> 00:17:23.410
would erupt in such chaos.

00:17:23.410 --> 00:17:27.960
So every thinking happens
in comparison, is that I,

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:31.090
it was better back then or it was better,

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:32.600
more wars before.

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:34.334
So yeah, it depends who you ask.

00:17:34.334 --> 00:17:35.620
There are people,

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:37.480
if you go to southern part of Iraq,

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:40.040
which there was kind of a semi Arab Spring

00:17:40.040 --> 00:17:42.860
happened in 1991, after
the first Gulf War.

00:17:42.860 --> 00:17:45.660
The usual houses you see
pictures of dead children,

00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:49.340
dead sons, and, I think
these guys have a different

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:51.870
perspective whether they
want Saddam to stay or not.

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:53.963
And if he stayed, and he was old,

00:17:54.830 --> 00:17:56.930
most likely his son would
have been the President,

00:17:56.930 --> 00:17:59.840
which is Uday Saddam
Hussein, who was far more,

00:17:59.840 --> 00:18:01.400
I mean if you think Saddam is evil,

00:18:01.400 --> 00:18:03.003
this guy's evil on steroids.

00:18:03.880 --> 00:18:04.713
So yeah.

00:18:06.103 --> 00:18:07.140
I struggle with these questions,

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:09.880
and I don't really have
the best answer to them,

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:12.750
but I think it's that, it
all depends what you value.

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:15.310
If what you value for
example, which I value,

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:17.353
is for example, freedom of information.

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:20.460
Yes, Iraq is doing much better

00:18:20.460 --> 00:18:21.550
in terms of freedom of information,

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:23.690
they have much more
accessibility to information.

00:18:23.690 --> 00:18:28.267
So if you want to democratize
or move the society

00:18:28.267 --> 00:18:31.210
to become more accepting
of enlightenment values,

00:18:31.210 --> 00:18:33.530
or any values that you'd like to promote,

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:38.040
Iraq is more suitable for
it now than it used to 2003,

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:41.030
the same way Syria, I mean
Syria now is under Assad,

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:42.410
is equivalent to what it used to be

00:18:42.410 --> 00:18:44.730
in terms of authoritarianism
and information.

00:18:44.730 --> 00:18:47.690
So this all depends what you
value, if you value safety,

00:18:47.690 --> 00:18:49.660
Saddam Hussein's time were much safer.

00:18:49.660 --> 00:18:51.060
But how did he kept us safe,

00:18:51.060 --> 00:18:53.160
by putting people in the gulag,

00:18:53.160 --> 00:18:54.237
the equivalent of a gulag,

00:18:54.237 --> 00:18:57.380
I mean that's, it's authoritarian safety,

00:18:57.380 --> 00:18:59.910
it's not like safety as in
everybody getting along.

00:18:59.910 --> 00:19:01.390
But it's more like if you speak your mind,

00:19:01.390 --> 00:19:02.250
you're gonna get killed.

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:03.650
So of course you're gonna get safety.

00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:04.483
(laughing)

00:19:04.483 --> 00:19:06.100
So it's...

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:07.380
- But you get a lot of pushback

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:08.810
when you say that sort of thing, right?

00:19:08.810 --> 00:19:10.570
When you say that Iraq now,

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:12.280
even though Iraq is
obviously still a mess,

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:13.700
and there was that brief moment,

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:15.100
and I do wanna focus on it a little bit,

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:17.720
that brief moment where there
were free and fair election,

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:19.640
you know, basically
free and fair elections.

00:19:19.640 --> 00:19:21.200
And the whole world saw it.

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:22.960
But then the US under Obama,

00:19:22.960 --> 00:19:24.310
just pulled out just like that,

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:25.750
and then things got much worse, but,

00:19:25.750 --> 00:19:28.800
at that little moment, when
there were the elections,

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:30.523
do you think more people were
thinking like you just said?

00:19:30.523 --> 00:19:32.903
Because I see you say this
sort of thing right now,

00:19:32.903 --> 00:19:35.520
that America has sort of
freed Iraq in a lot of ways.

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:37.570
And then I see the amount
of hate that you get

00:19:37.570 --> 00:19:41.073
from, usually unfortunately
from the lefties.

00:19:41.073 --> 00:19:44.180
- Again, so, I mean, I'm,

00:19:44.180 --> 00:19:48.120
as I grow older, quote unquote, is that,

00:19:48.120 --> 00:19:51.655
I think it's that even these
fair elections at the time

00:19:51.655 --> 00:19:54.300
were not necessarily the best idea.

00:19:54.300 --> 00:19:57.420
Because this is a country
that was devastated

00:19:57.420 --> 00:20:00.420
by multiple wars, many of the
people are either sectarian

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:04.223
or live in less education, so,

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:08.340
in a way the fair
elections, or the elections,

00:20:08.340 --> 00:20:10.750
has enhanced sectarianism
more than it reduces,

00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:13.040
because many of the people
who are Shias that say,

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:15.470
voted for Shia parties, and
Sunnis voted for Sunni parties,

00:20:15.470 --> 00:20:16.890
and Kurds voted for the Kurds.

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:21.890
So, as I now think about
it is that democracy,

00:20:22.130 --> 00:20:23.900
which I think, that's
where I think the Iraq War

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:27.000
has failed miserably, is that democracy's

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:28.110
not just about elections.

00:20:28.110 --> 00:20:30.280
It's about institutions,
it's about civil liberties,

00:20:30.280 --> 00:20:33.328
it's about all of these good education,

00:20:33.328 --> 00:20:34.950
good infrastructure.

00:20:34.950 --> 00:20:36.800
That was not, I mean,

00:20:36.800 --> 00:20:39.040
and that was not built, even though,

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:41.720
some may argue that was the
intention of the war, but,

00:20:41.720 --> 00:20:44.670
as you dig in more and you
see the corruption between

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:46.850
the contractors and the other contractors,

00:20:46.850 --> 00:20:49.530
and suddenly just the money
disappears for some reason,

00:20:49.530 --> 00:20:52.980
which was all meant for kind
of rebuilding institutions.

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:55.810
That is I think what was really missing.

00:20:55.810 --> 00:20:58.560
- But do you think, were we
on the path until we left,

00:20:58.560 --> 00:21:00.350
like, so you had the elections,
I get what you're saying,

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:02.640
it still could create a sectarian mess.

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:03.700
'Cause everyone's just gonna vote

00:21:03.700 --> 00:21:05.690
for their own home parties, let's say.

00:21:05.690 --> 00:21:07.510
But were we sort of on the path,

00:21:07.510 --> 00:21:08.510
and then just magically left
(snaps)

00:21:08.510 --> 00:21:10.088
and then it got worse?

00:21:10.088 --> 00:21:12.090
- I mean the role was, I think,

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:14.090
even Democrats that I've talked to

00:21:14.090 --> 00:21:17.022
and who were supportive
think it's a disaster.

00:21:17.022 --> 00:21:20.519
Is that, I mean one of
the things that the,

00:21:20.519 --> 00:21:22.650
I was at the time in Iraq as well,

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:26.500
is that the US was doing
with there is a movement

00:21:26.500 --> 00:21:29.030
called the Awakening forces,
which were Sunni militias

00:21:29.030 --> 00:21:30.340
fighting against Al Qaeda.

00:21:30.340 --> 00:21:32.320
It was kind of acting as a neutral force

00:21:32.320 --> 00:21:33.610
between all of these.

00:21:33.610 --> 00:21:35.820
Because the Shias don't
see the Americans as Shias

00:21:35.820 --> 00:21:37.786
or Sunnis, they see them as Americans.

00:21:37.786 --> 00:21:39.330
Or vice versa.

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:40.163
- [Dave] Right.

00:21:40.163 --> 00:21:42.360
- So the tribes, for
example, were willing more,

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:44.933
the Sunni tribes were more
willing to work with Americans

00:21:44.933 --> 00:21:48.240
than they were willing to
work with the Iraqi Shias

00:21:48.240 --> 00:21:49.190
'cause they think the Iraqi Shias

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:51.810
are affiliated with Iran,
which is their eternal enemy.

00:21:51.810 --> 00:21:56.180
So in a way America was acting
as a kind of a neutral force

00:21:56.180 --> 00:21:58.580
that keeps all of these people together.

00:21:58.580 --> 00:22:01.700
And the moment that disappeared,

00:22:01.700 --> 00:22:04.640
which is from the withdrawal
which was amazingly unplanned,

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:06.970
the Iraqi government which
was sectarian at the time,

00:22:06.970 --> 00:22:08.650
and in a way still is,

00:22:08.650 --> 00:22:10.530
it mainly pulled out the
funding from all of the Sunnis

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:13.194
that were fighting against Al Qaeda.

00:22:13.194 --> 00:22:14.467
- [Dave] Right.

00:22:14.467 --> 00:22:16.760
- So when that happened, immediately,

00:22:16.760 --> 00:22:19.210
these guys who were
fighting against Al Qaeda,

00:22:19.210 --> 00:22:22.475
either becoming a target to
still the sleeping cells,

00:22:22.475 --> 00:22:25.263
sleeper cells of the terrorist group, or,

00:22:27.677 --> 00:22:30.110
they're like, okay, what are
we gonna fight for any more?

00:22:30.110 --> 00:22:32.070
So then suddenly ISIS comes up.

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:33.404
What a surprise.

00:22:33.404 --> 00:22:35.350
So for those who actually followed Iraq

00:22:35.350 --> 00:22:37.620
from all of the beginning to the end,

00:22:37.620 --> 00:22:39.920
the ISIS evolution makes absolute sense.

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:42.390
- Right, and there's
probably a parable here to,

00:22:42.390 --> 00:22:44.410
in a way, what's happening right now

00:22:44.410 --> 00:22:47.690
with Turkey and the
Kurds, that we were there.

00:22:47.690 --> 00:22:48.710
Maybe we shouldn't have been there,

00:22:48.710 --> 00:22:49.980
you can make all those arguments.

00:22:49.980 --> 00:22:51.670
But we were there and
now we moved these troops

00:22:51.670 --> 00:22:54.050
and now Turkey's just
slaughtering these Kurds,

00:22:54.050 --> 00:22:56.100
and it's like, alright, so
maybe if we did try to go

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:58.550
for good reasons, you know,
like, putting that debate aside.

00:22:58.550 --> 00:22:59.517
- Yeah yeah.

00:22:59.517 --> 00:23:00.750
- We were there, we know
that the Kurds were basically

00:23:00.750 --> 00:23:01.930
our allies there.

00:23:01.930 --> 00:23:05.347
And now we've left just
like we sorta left there.

00:23:05.347 --> 00:23:07.239
- And now the Kurds want Americans back

00:23:07.239 --> 00:23:09.427
to defend them from all
of the other factions--

00:23:09.427 --> 00:23:12.460
- And is that our
responsibility, those are...

00:23:12.460 --> 00:23:14.120
- I mean I do think--
- Deep questions.

00:23:14.120 --> 00:23:17.330
- I do think it is, I mean,

00:23:17.330 --> 00:23:21.830
I think people on, let's
called it the liberal world,

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:26.830
is that we have to support
our fellow liberals.

00:23:27.050 --> 00:23:28.740
Or people at least adhere to some,

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:31.060
I mean in Kurdish factions
there's communists

00:23:31.060 --> 00:23:33.185
and there are other
factions that are there.

00:23:33.185 --> 00:23:34.018
- When you say liberal,
just to clean it up.

00:23:34.018 --> 00:23:36.030
So you mean, do you
mean just basic Western

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:36.863
ideas of freedom?

00:23:36.863 --> 00:23:39.636
- People, who yes, support
freedom in general,

00:23:39.636 --> 00:23:42.320
and adhere to women's
equality and all of that.

00:23:42.320 --> 00:23:47.320
So there is a funny quote
that's from a Lebanese poet,

00:23:47.530 --> 00:23:51.230
he said, Sunnis have Saudi
Arabia, Shias have Iran.

00:23:51.230 --> 00:23:53.271
But secularists have nobody but God.

00:23:53.271 --> 00:23:54.856
(laughing)

00:23:54.856 --> 00:23:55.892
- That's pretty good.

00:23:55.892 --> 00:23:57.892
- So in a ways that,

00:23:57.892 --> 00:24:01.780
if we do not support those
who adhere to these values,

00:24:01.780 --> 00:24:03.760
at least some of them, compared to others,

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:05.680
I'm not sure that the YPJ and others

00:24:05.680 --> 00:24:08.430
adhere to Western values
or enlightenment values.

00:24:08.430 --> 00:24:12.840
But they are much better than
some of the other factions.

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:14.600
I mean it's all by comparison, so,

00:24:14.600 --> 00:24:16.740
if we're gonna leave these
guys to be slaughtered,

00:24:16.740 --> 00:24:19.240
then who do we have left to support?

00:24:19.240 --> 00:24:23.033
That is one of the, is that
we need people on the ground--

00:24:23.033 --> 00:24:25.590
- Well the libertarian, the
purely libertarian argument

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:27.530
would be just get the
hell out, let them do it,

00:24:27.530 --> 00:24:28.363
and that's that.

00:24:28.363 --> 00:24:32.080
- I always say, is that the Middle East

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:33.500
is the opposite of Las Vegas.

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:34.490
What happens in the Middle East

00:24:34.490 --> 00:24:35.970
doesn't stay in the Middle East.

00:24:35.970 --> 00:24:38.510
So, you cannot leave this region alone.

00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:39.870
It's for, I mean,

00:24:39.870 --> 00:24:42.000
I just came back from
Europe and I see a lot

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:45.830
of the refugees coming from
Syria and other war zones.

00:24:45.830 --> 00:24:48.820
And this is an area that is the central,

00:24:48.820 --> 00:24:49.950
it's called the Middle East,

00:24:49.950 --> 00:24:51.810
it's in the middle of the world.

00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:55.030
And it is the birthplace of
all the monotheistic religions.

00:24:55.030 --> 00:24:57.650
And its influence, I mean,

00:24:57.650 --> 00:24:59.980
it's also one of the
richest parts of the world

00:24:59.980 --> 00:25:01.450
because of oil and natural gas.

00:25:01.450 --> 00:25:06.370
And we cannot be left alone.

00:25:06.370 --> 00:25:07.951
And I mean,

00:25:07.951 --> 00:25:09.840
the terrorists or the extremists

00:25:09.840 --> 00:25:11.280
are not the ones leaving us alone.

00:25:11.280 --> 00:25:13.110
So the thing is that, if you say,

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:14.040
I'm gonna leave them alone,

00:25:14.040 --> 00:25:17.040
it is on the premise that
there's kind of an agreement,

00:25:17.040 --> 00:25:19.440
a mutually assured destruction
here, but there's not.

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:20.700
I mean when you have a guy

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:22.670
who wants to establish a caliphate,

00:25:22.670 --> 00:25:24.440
and you're like, no, it's not my business,

00:25:24.440 --> 00:25:25.580
this is a bad deal.

00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:26.413
- [Dave] Right.

00:25:26.413 --> 00:25:29.826
- So the extremists are expansionists.

00:25:29.826 --> 00:25:31.150
The extremists are not gonna be happy,

00:25:31.150 --> 00:25:33.390
I mean even ISIS, they
were not happy with just,

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:35.230
even though they get a large territory,

00:25:35.230 --> 00:25:37.030
but they were not happy
with just this territory,

00:25:37.030 --> 00:25:40.399
they were willing to
expand, they have ads up--

00:25:40.399 --> 00:25:42.390
- They don't get to a border and be like,

00:25:42.390 --> 00:25:43.621
alright, we're good to go.

00:25:43.621 --> 00:25:44.580
- Yeah.

00:25:44.580 --> 00:25:48.019
They are anti, them and
the libertarians (laughing)

00:25:48.019 --> 00:25:49.570
have in common with them in which like

00:25:49.570 --> 00:25:51.670
it's a world that is
borderless, is that this,

00:25:51.670 --> 00:25:54.850
they see that their ideology
is borderless, which is,

00:25:54.850 --> 00:25:56.930
which is true according
to their interpretation.

00:25:56.930 --> 00:25:58.240
So yeah.

00:25:58.240 --> 00:26:01.869
So I think it's that
leave them alone policy.

00:26:01.869 --> 00:26:04.430
And also is like, I mean, on what premise?

00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:06.310
I mean America has
intervened in the region,

00:26:06.310 --> 00:26:09.701
so it's just like you intervene
and then you leave it.

00:26:09.701 --> 00:26:10.710
In this situation--

00:26:10.710 --> 00:26:12.530
- If you break it, you fix it.

00:26:12.530 --> 00:26:16.650
- Yes, I do, and how to
maintain all of that world order

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:18.780
with all of, if you're
not gonna intervene,

00:26:18.780 --> 00:26:20.970
the Russians will, as happened in Syria.

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:22.820
And if you're not gonna
intervene, the Turks will.

00:26:22.820 --> 00:26:24.840
And which world order
do you want to live in?

00:26:24.840 --> 00:26:26.880
- Just very quickly on the Kurds.

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:30.820
They basically have, the KRG
basically has a state in Iraq.

00:26:30.820 --> 00:26:31.730
Pretty much.

00:26:31.730 --> 00:26:32.890
- [Faisal] And autonomous, yes.

00:26:32.890 --> 00:26:35.690
- Would you just be for
carving out that piece of Iraq

00:26:35.690 --> 00:26:37.460
officially and just letting
them have that state?

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:38.920
I understand that you
still have the Syrian Kurds

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:40.060
and the Turks and all the rest.

00:26:40.060 --> 00:26:42.470
But do you think as an
Iraqi, do you think that that

00:26:42.470 --> 00:26:44.020
basically would just be fine?

00:26:44.020 --> 00:26:45.720
I mean, it sort of is.

00:26:45.720 --> 00:26:48.630
- I mean I think this all
depends on what they want.

00:26:48.630 --> 00:26:50.451
I mean there are arguments,

00:26:50.451 --> 00:26:54.320
and I'm happily an ally
of many Kurds, and,

00:26:54.320 --> 00:26:58.210
their arguments on, some
argue that we have to stay

00:26:58.210 --> 00:26:59.230
as a part of Iraq.

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:01.400
And some of them argue that
they have to be autonomous.

00:27:01.400 --> 00:27:04.500
And there are, because there's
a lot of natural resources

00:27:04.500 --> 00:27:05.487
that exist in Iraq that kind of,

00:27:05.487 --> 00:27:08.600
that they can rely on and benefit from.

00:27:08.600 --> 00:27:11.350
And also, if you can form a state,

00:27:11.350 --> 00:27:13.400
you also might become vulnerable.

00:27:13.400 --> 00:27:16.250
At least now, Iraq is
a state of some sort.

00:27:16.250 --> 00:27:18.560
So that if there's a Turkish invasion.

00:27:18.560 --> 00:27:21.760
It'd still be a Turkish invasion
of Iraq in which, where,

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:23.830
it's the state versus a Kurdish state

00:27:23.830 --> 00:27:25.200
that they will be vulnerable.

00:27:25.200 --> 00:27:26.060
- Is that true though?

00:27:26.060 --> 00:27:29.710
Would the Iraqi army defend, you think,

00:27:29.710 --> 00:27:32.460
if the KRG, the area got attacked?

00:27:32.460 --> 00:27:34.140
You think the Iraqi army would step in?

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:37.016
- Yes and maybe even the US army, right?

00:27:37.016 --> 00:27:37.890
I mean the US embassy in Iraq

00:27:37.890 --> 00:27:39.390
is the largest embassy in the world.

00:27:39.390 --> 00:27:41.060
So it's still, there is still--

00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:41.893
- Still part of it.

00:27:41.893 --> 00:27:44.713
- There's still infrastructure
that they can depend on.

00:27:46.060 --> 00:27:47.860
I mean, maybe this is,

00:27:47.860 --> 00:27:52.693
me and extremists agree
on it, but I am for unity.

00:27:54.037 --> 00:27:56.380
I agree that there needs
to be nation states

00:27:56.380 --> 00:27:58.050
and there needs to be, in a way,

00:27:58.050 --> 00:27:59.670
states that are based on ethnic groups,

00:27:59.670 --> 00:28:02.530
because of the fact that
they're being persecuted.

00:28:02.530 --> 00:28:04.970
But at the same time, it's,

00:28:04.970 --> 00:28:06.500
I mean there are a lot of issues

00:28:06.500 --> 00:28:09.740
that the world is now being
more interconnected than ever,

00:28:09.740 --> 00:28:12.246
and we have to, so what happens in Iraq,

00:28:12.246 --> 00:28:14.810
and what happens in other states
all connect to each other,

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:19.624
so I'm more in favor of
more unity than division.

00:28:19.624 --> 00:28:21.950
I mean we kind of created division, okay,

00:28:21.950 --> 00:28:22.900
the Kurds have their state.

00:28:22.900 --> 00:28:24.910
How about the Shias, should
they have their state?

00:28:24.910 --> 00:28:25.830
Should they be with Iran,

00:28:25.830 --> 00:28:27.020
or should they be a separate state,

00:28:27.020 --> 00:28:28.325
or how about the Sunnis,

00:28:28.325 --> 00:28:29.158
should they be with Saudi Arabia?

00:28:29.158 --> 00:28:29.991
Like there's always gonna be--

00:28:29.991 --> 00:28:31.470
- We think we're divided in America.

00:28:31.470 --> 00:28:32.837
That must be hilarious to you.

00:28:32.837 --> 00:28:34.950
- Yeah, so who is gonna
be a representative?

00:28:34.950 --> 00:28:36.570
- People are arguing
about marginal tax rates,

00:28:36.570 --> 00:28:37.403
and you're like, come on.

00:28:37.403 --> 00:28:39.380
This is not sectarian, but real quick.

00:28:39.380 --> 00:28:42.190
I think we have to bizarrely
give Hillary Clinton

00:28:42.190 --> 00:28:44.570
a shout out here, because,
when did you move to America,

00:28:44.570 --> 00:28:45.859
in 2009, right?

00:28:45.859 --> 00:28:46.942
- 2000, no, 2013.

00:28:46.942 --> 00:28:48.060
- 2013.

00:28:48.060 --> 00:28:49.220
- Yeah, I left Iraq in 2009.

00:28:49.220 --> 00:28:52.140
- So during the campaign,
the first Obama campaign,

00:28:52.140 --> 00:28:55.780
when they were in the
primary, Hillary versus Obama,

00:28:55.780 --> 00:28:57.010
I don't know if you saw
this from the outside,

00:28:57.010 --> 00:28:59.020
but Hillary kept saying
we have to leave Iraq,

00:28:59.020 --> 00:29:00.630
but we have to do it responsibly,

00:29:00.630 --> 00:29:02.570
with a timeline, and this whole thing.

00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:04.861
And Obama kept saying no,
we've gotta go, we've gotta go,

00:29:04.861 --> 00:29:06.710
and that was in the midst of
the hope and change thing.

00:29:06.710 --> 00:29:10.440
And just everybody just
like drinking the Kool-Aid.

00:29:10.440 --> 00:29:12.200
And then he did do it,
and then as you're saying,

00:29:12.200 --> 00:29:14.490
it led to a lot of those
things, so in a weird way,

00:29:14.490 --> 00:29:16.300
Hillary actually deserves some credit

00:29:16.300 --> 00:29:20.320
for being honest, at least,
about what should have been done

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:23.100
as opposed to just the rash
decision to just get going.

00:29:23.100 --> 00:29:24.910
- And then at the end of the day,

00:29:24.910 --> 00:29:26.300
it'll help that we're gonna talk

00:29:26.300 --> 00:29:27.133
about modernization in a bit,

00:29:27.133 --> 00:29:29.930
is that I think that
at the end of the day,

00:29:29.930 --> 00:29:32.090
people in the Middle East
have to fix their own region.

00:29:32.090 --> 00:29:35.790
I mean I'm not in favor of
interventionism forever,

00:29:35.790 --> 00:29:38.450
or even interventionism in some cases.

00:29:38.450 --> 00:29:41.700
At the end of the day,
the people of the region

00:29:41.700 --> 00:29:42.930
have to choose their own destiny,

00:29:42.930 --> 00:29:46.200
and that destiny should not
be antagonistic to the world.

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:50.250
And so, which brings up the need that

00:29:50.250 --> 00:29:54.150
we cannot rely, I mean I
think now is more than ever

00:29:54.150 --> 00:29:55.690
and even previously,

00:29:55.690 --> 00:29:57.800
the United States is
not a reliable partner.

00:29:57.800 --> 00:29:59.610
If you are fighting in the region,

00:29:59.610 --> 00:30:01.630
the United States might
switch left to right,

00:30:01.630 --> 00:30:04.652
and some people might use interventionism

00:30:04.652 --> 00:30:06.915
as an argument for getting votes,

00:30:06.915 --> 00:30:08.870
and some people might get
it to not getting votes.

00:30:08.870 --> 00:30:10.910
At the end of the day
you cannot rely on that.

00:30:10.910 --> 00:30:13.690
And it is in a way a weakness
of the United States,

00:30:13.690 --> 00:30:16.760
because as a democracy, you
can have all these shifts.

00:30:16.760 --> 00:30:19.370
Well for example with Russia,
I was having a call with,

00:30:19.370 --> 00:30:20.520
with actually a Syrian,

00:30:21.470 --> 00:30:24.720
not militia, but he's kind of an observer.

00:30:24.720 --> 00:30:26.140
And it was like, at
least with the Russians,

00:30:26.140 --> 00:30:28.380
I know what's gonna happen
in the next 10 years.

00:30:28.380 --> 00:30:29.460
With the Chinese I know
what's going to happen

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:30.770
in the next 15 years.

00:30:30.770 --> 00:30:32.280
But with the Americans,

00:30:32.280 --> 00:30:34.640
okay, we can have an
interventionist President like Bush,

00:30:34.640 --> 00:30:36.970
for example, and then you
can have Obama after that.

00:30:36.970 --> 00:30:39.740
And now I don't know what
the shitshow is, but--

00:30:39.740 --> 00:30:43.021
- It's an interesting sort
of soft spot of democracy,

00:30:43.021 --> 00:30:44.111
somehow, yeah.

00:30:44.111 --> 00:30:46.925
- And I mean the problems
in many of these issues,

00:30:46.925 --> 00:30:48.900
of extremism, authoritarian,

00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:50.730
these all need long term solutions.

00:30:50.730 --> 00:30:52.930
I mean even for example, which is kind of,

00:30:52.930 --> 00:30:55.670
we rely that as a basic
of what people ask us

00:30:55.670 --> 00:30:58.290
about theory of change,
is that in the Cold War,

00:30:58.290 --> 00:31:02.220
